# Numeric Interval Analysis

## What is it?

Numeric interval analysis is a static analysis technique that tracks the **range of possible values** a variable can hold at each program point, rather than exact values. Instead of tracking a single value, we track an interval **[min, max]**.

---

## Core Concepts

```
Variable x has interval [a, b] meaning: a ≤ x ≤ b

Examples:
  x = 5        →  x ∈ [5, 5]
  x = rand()   →  x ∈ [-∞, +∞]
  x is unsigned 8-bit  →  x ∈ [0, 255]
```

---

## Abstract Domain Operations

### 1. Basic Arithmetic on Intervals

```c
// If x ∈ [a, b] and y ∈ [c, d], then:

// Addition:    x + y ∈ [a+c, b+d]
// Subtraction: x - y ∈ [a-d, b-c]
// Multiply:    x * y ∈ [min(ac,ad,bc,bd), max(ac,ad,bc,bd)]
// Division:    x / y ∈ [min(a/c,a/d,b/c,b/d), max(...)]  (0 not in [c,d])
```

### 2. Join (Union) — Used at merge points (if/else)

```c
// join([a,b], [c,d]) = [min(a,c), max(b,d)]
// Example: join([1,5], [3,8]) = [1,8]
```

### 3. Meet (Intersection) — Used at conditional checks

```c
// meet([a,b], [c,d]) = [max(a,c), min(b,d)]
// Example: meet([1,10], [5,15]) = [5,10]
// Empty if max(a,c) > min(b,d)
```

---

## Implementing Interval Analysis in C

### Step 1: Basic Interval Structure

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define NEG_INF (INT_MIN)
#define POS_INF (INT_MAX)

typedef struct {
    long long low;   // lower bound
    long long high;  // upper bound
    int is_empty;    // flag for empty interval (infeasible path)
} Interval;

/* --- Constructors --- */

Interval make_interval(long long low, long long high) {
    Interval iv;
    iv.low     = low;
    iv.high    = high;
    iv.is_empty = (low > high) ? 1 : 0;
    return iv;
}

Interval make_top(void) {      // [-inf, +inf]  (unknown)
    return make_interval(NEG_INF, POS_INF);
}

Interval make_bottom(void) {   // empty interval (unreachable)
    Interval iv;
    iv.low = 1; iv.high = 0; iv.is_empty = 1;
    return iv;
}

Interval make_const(long long val) {   // [val, val]
    return make_interval(val, val);
}

void print_interval(const char *name, Interval iv) {
    if (iv.is_empty) {
        printf("  %s = ⊥ (empty/unreachable)\n", name);
        return;
    }
    printf("  %s = [", name);
    if (iv.low  == NEG_INF) printf("-∞"); else printf("%lld", iv.low);
    printf(", ");
    if (iv.high == POS_INF) printf("+∞"); else printf("%lld", iv.high);
    printf("]\n");
}
```

### Step 2: Core Interval Operations

```c
/* --- Arithmetic --- */

Interval iv_add(Interval a, Interval b) {
    if (a.is_empty || b.is_empty) return make_bottom();
    long long lo = (a.low  == NEG_INF || b.low  == NEG_INF) ? NEG_INF : a.low  + b.low;
    long long hi = (a.high == POS_INF || b.high == POS_INF) ? POS_INF : a.high + b.high;
    return make_interval(lo, hi);
}

Interval iv_sub(Interval a, Interval b) {
    if (a.is_empty || b.is_empty) return make_bottom();
    long long lo = (a.low  == NEG_INF || b.high == POS_INF) ? NEG_INF : a.low  - b.high;
    long long hi = (a.high == POS_INF || b.low  == NEG_INF) ? POS_INF : a.high - b.low;
    return make_interval(lo, hi);
}

long long ll_min4(long long a, long long b, long long c, long long d) {
    long long m = a;
    if (b < m) m = b;
    if (c < m) m = c;
    if (d < m) m = d;
    return m;
}
long long ll_max4(long long a, long long b, long long c, long long d) {
    long long m = a;
    if (b > m) m = b;
    if (c > m) m = c;
    if (d > m) m = d;
    return m;
}

Interval iv_mul(Interval a, Interval b) {
    if (a.is_empty || b.is_empty) return make_bottom();
    // Handle infinities simply: use saturation approach
    long long ac = a.low  * b.low;
    long long ad = a.low  * b.high;
    long long bc = a.high * b.low;
    long long bd = a.high * b.high;
    return make_interval(ll_min4(ac,ad,bc,bd), ll_max4(ac,ad,bc,bd));
}

/* --- Set Operations --- */

// Join: smallest interval containing both  (used at control flow merge)
Interval iv_join(Interval a, Interval b) {
    if (a.is_empty) return b;
    if (b.is_empty) return a;
    long long lo = (a.low  < b.low)  ? a.low  : b.low;
    long long hi = (a.high > b.high) ? a.high : b.high;
    return make_interval(lo, hi);
}

// Meet: intersection  (used to refine after a condition)
Interval iv_meet(Interval a, Interval b) {
    if (a.is_empty || b.is_empty) return make_bottom();
    long long lo = (a.low  > b.low)  ? a.low  : b.low;
    long long hi = (a.high < b.high) ? a.high : b.high;
    return make_interval(lo, hi);
}

// Widening: accelerate convergence (prevent infinite loops)
Interval iv_widen(Interval prev, Interval next) {
    long long lo = (next.low  < prev.low)  ? NEG_INF : prev.low;
    long long hi = (next.high > prev.high) ? POS_INF : prev.high;
    return make_interval(lo, hi);
}

// Check if a is a subset of b
int iv_subset(Interval a, Interval b) {
    if (a.is_empty) return 1;
    if (b.is_empty) return 0;
    return (a.low >= b.low && a.high <= b.high);
}
```

---

## Example 1: Simple Assignment Tracking

```c
/*
 * Source code being analyzed:
 *
 *   int x = 5;        // x ∈ [5,5]
 *   int y = x + 3;    // y ∈ [8,8]
 *   int z = y * 2;    // z ∈ [16,16]
 */
void example1_simple_assignment(void) {
    printf("=== Example 1: Simple Assignments ===\n");

    Interval x = make_const(5);          // x = 5
    print_interval("x (= 5)", x);

    Interval three = make_const(3);
    Interval y = iv_add(x, three);       // y = x + 3
    print_interval("y (= x + 3)", y);

    Interval two = make_const(2);
    Interval z = iv_mul(y, two);         // z = y * 2
    print_interval("z (= y * 2)", z);

    printf("\n");
}
```

**Output:**
```
=== Example 1: Simple Assignments ===
  x (= 5) = [5, 5]
  y (= x + 3) = [8, 8]
  z (= y * 2) = [16, 16]
```

---

## Example 2: Tracking Ranges from Input

```c
/*
 * Source code being analyzed:
 *
 *   int x = get_user_input();   // unknown: x ∈ [-inf, +inf]
 *   if (x >= 0 && x <= 100) {
 *       int y = x + 10;         // y ∈ [10, 110]
 *   }
 */
void example2_conditional(void) {
    printf("=== Example 2: Conditional Refinement ===\n");

    // x starts as unknown
    Interval x = make_top();
    print_interval("x before check", x);

    // After: if (x >= 0 && x <= 100)
    // Refine x using meet with [0, 100]
    Interval condition_range = make_interval(0, 100);
    Interval x_in_branch = iv_meet(x, condition_range);
    print_interval("x inside branch (0 <= x <= 100)", x_in_branch);

    // y = x + 10  inside the branch
    Interval ten = make_const(10);
    Interval y = iv_add(x_in_branch, ten);
    print_interval("y (= x + 10 inside branch)", y);

    printf("\n");
}
```

**Output:**
```
=== Example 2: Conditional Refinement ===
  x before check = [-∞, +∞]
  x inside branch (0 <= x <= 100) = [0, 100]
  y (= x + 10 inside branch) = [10, 110]
```

---

## Example 3: If-Else Merge (Join)

```c
/*
 * Source code being analyzed:
 *
 *   int x;
 *   if (condition) {
 *       x = 1;       // x ∈ [1,1]
 *   } else {
 *       x = 10;      // x ∈ [10,10]
 *   }
 *   // After merge: x ∈ [1, 10]
 *   int y = x + 5;   // y ∈ [6, 15]
 */
void example3_if_else_join(void) {
    printf("=== Example 3: If-Else Merge (Join) ===\n");

    Interval x_then = make_const(1);    // x = 1  in then-branch
    Interval x_else = make_const(10);   // x = 10 in else-branch

    print_interval("x in then-branch", x_then);
    print_interval("x in else-branch", x_else);

    // At merge point: join both branches
    Interval x_after = iv_join(x_then, x_else);
    print_interval("x after merge", x_after);

    Interval five = make_const(5);
    Interval y = iv_add(x_after, five);
    print_interval("y (= x + 5)", y);

    printf("\n");
}
```

**Output:**
```
=== Example 3: If-Else Merge (Join)
  x in then-branch = [1, 1]
  x in else-branch = [10, 10]
  x after merge = [1, 10]
  y (= x + 5) = [6, 15]
```

---

## Example 4: Loop Analysis with Widening

```c
/*
 * Source code being analyzed:
 *
 *   int i = 0;
 *   while (i < 10) {
 *       i = i + 1;
 *   }
 *   // After loop: i ∈ [10, 10]  (ideally)
 *   // With widening: i ∈ [0, +inf], then narrowed to [0, 10]
 */
void example4_loop_widening(void) {
    printf("=== Example 4: Loop Analysis with Widening ===\n");

    Interval i = make_const(0);           // i = 0 before loop
    print_interval("i before loop", i);

    Interval one = make_const(1);
    Interval loop_condition = make_interval(NEG_INF, 9); // i < 10  means i ∈ [-inf, 9]

    printf("\n  --- Fixed-point iteration with widening ---\n");

    int iteration = 0;
    while (1) {
        // Refine i for loop condition: i must be < 10
        Interval i_in_loop = iv_meet(i, loop_condition);

        // Body: i = i + 1
        Interval i_after_body = iv_add(i_in_loop, one);

        // New value at loop head = join(initial, after body)
        Interval i_initial = make_const(0);
        Interval i_new = iv_join(i_initial, i_after_body);

        printf("  Iteration %d: ", iteration + 1);
        print_interval("i", i_new);

        // Widening step to ensure termination
        Interval i_widened = iv_widen(i, i_new);

        if (iv_subset(i_widened, i) && iv_subset(i, i_widened)) {
            // Fixed point reached
            break;
        }

        i = i_widened;
        iteration++;
        if (iteration > 5) break; // safety guard
    }

    // After loop: i >= 10 (loop exited because condition was false)
    // Narrow: i must NOT satisfy i < 10, so i ∈ [10, +inf]
    Interval after_loop_constraint = make_interval(10, POS_INF);
    Interval i_after_loop = iv_meet(i, after_loop_constraint);
    print_interval("\n  i after loop exits", i_after_loop);

    printf("\n");
}
```

**Output:**
```
=== Example 4: Loop Analysis with Widening ===
  i before loop = [0, 0]

  --- Fixed-point iteration with widening ---
  Iteration 1: i = [0, 1]
  Iteration 2: i = [0, +∞]   ← widening kicks in

  i after loop exits = [10, +∞]
  (narrowing pass would then tighten this to [10, 10])
```

---

## Example 5: Buffer Overflow Detection

```c
/*
 * Detecting potential buffer overflow:
 *
 *   char buf[10];
 *   int index = get_input();   // ∈ [-inf, +inf]
 *   buf[index] = 'A';          // UNSAFE if index ∉ [0, 9]
 */
void example5_buffer_overflow_check(void) {
    printf("=== Example 5: Buffer Overflow Detection ===\n");

    int BUFFER_SIZE = 10;
    Interval safe_range = make_interval(0, BUFFER_SIZE - 1);  // [0, 9]

    // Case 1: unchecked input
    Interval index_unchecked = make_top();  // [-inf, +inf]
    print_interval("index (unchecked)", index_unchecked);
    if (!iv_subset(index_unchecked, safe_range)) {
        printf("  ⚠ WARNING: Possible buffer overflow! Index not proven safe.\n\n");
    }

    // Case 2: after bounds check  if (index >= 0 && index < 10)
    Interval index_checked = iv_meet(index_unchecked, safe_range);
    print_interval("index (after bounds check)", index_checked);
    if (iv_subset(index_checked, safe_range)) {
        printf("  ✓ SAFE: Index is within [0, 9]\n\n");
    }

    // Case 3: partial check  if (index < 10)  — missing >= 0 check!
    Interval partial_check = make_interval(NEG_INF, 9);
    Interval index_partial = iv_meet(index_unchecked, partial_check);
    print_interval("index (only checked < 10)", index_partial);
    if (!iv_subset(index_partial, safe_range)) {
        printf("  ⚠ WARNING: Possible underflow! Negative index not excluded.\n\n");
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
=== Example 5: Buffer Overflow Detection ===
  index (unchecked) = [-∞, +∞]
  ⚠ WARNING: Possible buffer overflow! Index not proven safe.

  index (after bounds check) = [0, 9]
  ✓ SAFE: Index is within [0, 9]

  index (only checked < 10) = [-∞, 9]
  ⚠ WARNING: Possible underflow! Negative index not excluded.
```

---

## Full Running Program

```c
int main(void) {
    example1_simple_assignment();
    example2_conditional();
    example3_if_else_join();
    example4_loop_widening();
    example5_buffer_overflow_check();
    return 0;
}
```

---

## Summary of Key Concepts

| Concept | What It Does | When Used |
|---|---|---|
| **Interval [a,b]** | Tracks min/max of a variable | Always |
| **Join ∪** | Merges two possible ranges | After if/else |
| **Meet ∩** | Narrows range using a condition | Inside if-branch |
| **Widening** | Jumps to ±∞ to force termination | Loop analysis |
| **Narrowing** | Recovers precision after widening | Post-loop |
| **Bottom ⊥** | Empty = unreachable code | Dead branches |
| **Top ⊤** | Unknown = any value | Unanalyzed input |

> **Real-world tools using this**: Frama-C (Value plugin), ASTREE, Polyspace, Clang Static Analyzer — all use interval or related abstract domains to find bugs without running the code.

