    <h2>About Borscht</h2>
    <p>
        Borscht (Ukrainian: <em>борщ</em>, romanized: <em>borshch</em>) is one of the most iconic dishes in Ukrainian cuisine
        and a source of immense cultural pride. This hearty, vibrantly red soup is built around beetroot and is typically
        enriched with cabbage, potatoes, carrots, tomatoes, and meat — most often beef or pork — and finished with a
        generous dollop of sour cream (<em>smetana</em>).
    </p>
    <p>
        In 2022, UNESCO inscribed the culture of Ukrainian borscht-making on its List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in
        Need of Urgent Safeguarding, recognizing both its cultural importance and the threat posed by the ongoing war in Ukraine.
        The dish is more than food — it is a symbol of Ukrainian national identity.
    </p>

    <div class="history-box">
        <h3>📜 A Brief History</h3>
        <p>
            The origins of borscht trace back to ancient Slavic peoples who made sour soups from hogweed
            (a plant called <em>borshchevik</em>, which gave the soup its name). Over centuries, beets became the dominant
            ingredient. By the 17th and 18th centuries, borscht was a staple in Ukrainian households across all social classes.
            Today, every Ukrainian family has its own treasured recipe passed down through generations.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Ingredients</h2>
    <div class="ingredients-grid">
        <div class="ingredient-group">
            <h3>🥩 For the Broth</h3>
            <ul class="ingredients">
                <li>500g (1 lb) beef short ribs or chuck</li>
                <li>2.5 liters (10 cups) cold water</li>
                <li>1 medium onion, halved</li>
                <li>1 bay leaf</li>
                <li>5 black peppercorns</li>
                <li>Salt to taste</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ingredient-group">
            <h3>🥬 Main Vegetables</h3>
            <ul class="ingredients">
                <li>3 medium beets, peeled</li>
                <li>3 medium potatoes, cubed</li>
                <li>¼ head of cabbage, shredded</li>
                <li>2 medium carrots, grated</li>
                <li>1 medium onion, diced</li>
                <li>2 stalks celery, chopped</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ingredient-group">
            <h3>🍅 Tomato Base</h3>
            <ul class="ingredients">
                <li>3 medium tomatoes, diced</li>
                <li>2 tbsp tomato paste</li>
                <li>1 tbsp apple cider vinegar</li>
                <li>1 tsp sugar</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ingredient-group">
            <h3>🧄 Aromatics & Finish</h3>
            <ul class="ingredients">
                <li>4 cloves garlic, minced</li>
                <li>3 tbsp sunflower or vegetable oil</li>
                <li>1 tbsp fresh dill, chopped</li>
                <li>1 tbsp fresh parsley, chopped</li>
                <li>Sour cream (<em>smetana</em>) to serve</li>
                <li>Salt &amp; black pepper to taste</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Instructions</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
        <li>
            <div>
                <strong>Make the broth:</strong> Place the beef in a large pot with cold water. Bring to a boil, skimming
                off any foam that rises to the surface. Add the halved onion, bay leaf, and peppercorns. Reduce heat and
                simmer for 60–90 minutes until the meat is tender. Remove the meat, shred it, and strain the broth. Discard
                the onion and bay leaf.
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <div>
                <strong>Prepare the beets:</strong> Grate or julienne 2 of the beets. In a skillet, heat 2 tbsp oil over
                medium heat. Sauté the beets for 5 minutes. Add tomato paste, vinegar, sugar, and a ladle of broth. Cook
                for another 8–10 minutes until the beets are soft and the mixture is deeply colored. Set aside. Grate or
                finely slice the third beet raw — this will be added at the end to preserve color.
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <div>
                <strong>Make the sofrito (zirvak):</strong> In the same skillet, heat the remaining oil. Add diced onion
                and carrots and cook over medium heat for 7–8 minutes until soft and golden. Add the diced tomatoes and
                cook another 5 minutes. Combine with the beet mixture.
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <div>
                <strong>Build the soup:</strong> Bring the strained broth back to a gentle boil. Add the cubed potatoes
                and cook for 10 minutes. Add the shredded cabbage and cook for another 5–7 minutes until just tender but
                not mushy.
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <div>
                <strong>Combine everything:</strong> Add the beet-tomato-vegetable mixture to the pot along with the
                shredded meat. Stir in the raw grated beet and minced garlic. Simmer for 5 more minutes. Taste and adjust
                salt, pepper, vinegar, and sugar to achieve the signature sweet-sour balance.
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <div>
                <strong>Rest and serve:</strong> Turn off the heat. Stir in fresh dill and parsley. Cover the pot and let
                the borscht rest for at least 15–20 minutes — it always tastes better after resting! Serve hot in deep
                bowls, topped with a generous spoonful of sour cream, extra fresh dill, and accompanied by dark rye bread
                or <a href="/recipes/ukrainian/pampushky">pampushky</a> (garlic bread rolls).
            </div>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="tips-box">
        <h3>💡 Pro Tips</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Preserve the red color:</strong> Add a splash of vinegar or lemon juice — the acid keeps beets vibrant and prevents the soup from turning brown.</li>
            <li><strong>Better the next day:</strong> Like most hearty soups, borscht deepens in flavor overnight. Make it a day ahead if you can.</li>
            <li><strong>The raw beet trick:</strong> Adding a small amount of raw grated beet at the very end boosts the color and fresh beet flavor significantly.</li>
            <li><strong>Fat matters:</strong> Traditional recipes often use lard (<em>salo</em>) instead of oil for a richer, more authentic flavor.</li>
            <li><strong>Don't skip the sour cream:</strong> It's not optional — it balances the earthy beet flavor and adds creaminess.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

